In Palmerton borough, where cars stop on the wide main street when you step off the curb and time seems to have frozen in the downtown, there's a new restaurant with city-style food and decor -- but the heart of a small town.
One Ten Tavern opened in February on Delaware Avenue, the borough's main street, after seven months of renovations. When I visited recently, the expansive front porch of this casual pub-style eatery extended a down-home autumn welcome with decorative flags, scarecrows and straw.
That same spirit greeted us as we walked through the doorway: Even before the door shut behind us, a server called out from the bar, "Hi, folks! How are you tonight?" Never have I experienced such a warm, exuberant welcome.
The dining room's decor, on the other hand, exuded a cooler, urbane sensibility. Subdued lighting intensified the mellow tones of wood paneling and the wood floor, buffed to a soft shine, that looks original to the building. Just a few tasteful prints reinforced the minimalist, rather modern style.
There was nothing small-town about the fine fare, either -- not with selections such as a hand-carved ribeye steak sandwich, the meat seasoned with black peppercorns and crushed garlic and chipotle mayonnaise, and sesame seed-crusted tuna loin served on mixed greens with Oriental ginger vinaigrette.
Chef Michael Lucas, who owns the restaurant with his wife, Sandra, features lots of standards on his menu, such as enchiladas, tacos, grilled chicken wings, burgers, crab cakes, shrimp cocktail, pizza and smoked pork chops. An extensive list of daily specials that focuses on seasonal ingredients from local markets augments the printed menu.
Nevertheless, it was the printed menu that we started from, considering that two appetizers -- crab fritters and seafood pierogies -- caught our attention.
