It`s a sign of the times: Several years ago, I was besieged with telephone phone calls about spaghetti squash, a vegetable that looks like a honeydew melon but seems like pasta.
''How do you cook it?'' was the frequent question.
In the years since, it appears that home cooks have learned how to handle it to their satisfaction because callers no longer ask about it. Three cheers for the resourcefulness of food lovers, who apparently have accepted it as part of the diet.
Two places where I have seen spaghetti squash lead me to believe that it is being accepted as more than a novelty food. One was the recent National Beef Cook-off in Wichita, Kan.: The contestant from Oklahoma used spaghetti squash halves as bowls in which the strands were fluffed with a fork and topped with a beef mixture. The entry was good enough to merit a $300 honorable mention.
If the vegetable is earning a place in our diets as a nice alternative to pasta, it seems logical that we could top it with just about any sauce we would make for pasta--from a simple oil, garlic and grated cheese topping to a complex long-simmering meat and vegetable mixture. Still, the slight sweetness of the squash makes it particularly good with simple accompaniment.
For those who still need to know how to cook it, here`s a skillet method that will work if you are cooking a small portion:
Halve the squash lengthwise and remove the seeds. Place it, cut side down, in a 12-inch skillet. Pour water to a depth of 2 inches into the skillet. Heat to a boil over high heat, then reduce heat to medium low; cover. Simmer 20 to 30 minutes or until tender, then drain. To serve, loosen squash from the shell and separate into strands with a fork.
The following recipe is from the ''Vegetables, The New Main Course Cookbook,'' by Joe Famularo and Louise Imperiale (Barron`s, $15.95).
SPAGHETTI SQUASH WITH SPINACH SHALLOTS AND BRANDY
Four servings
Preparation time: 25 minutes
Cooking time: 40 minutes
1 spaghetti squash, 2 to 3 pounds
2 cups shredded fresh spinach leaves, plus 8 large uncooked leaves
Juice of 1 lemon
5 tablespoons butter
1 garlic clove, finely chopped
2 tablespoons finely chopped shallots
1 cup thinly sliced mushrooms
2 tablespoons brandy
1 cup whipping cream
Salt, freshly ground pepper
6 bacon slices, cooked until crisp, drained and crumbled
1/4 cup freshly grated parmesan cheese
1. Halve the spaghetti squash lengthwise and remove seeds. Place squash in large pot with 3 inches of water, cover and boil for 25 minutes or until tender. Remove spaghetti squash with large spoon. Let cool slightly. Run fork over inside of squash to release flesh; it will resemble spaghetti strands.
(There should be about 2 cups.)
2. Cook shredded spinach in boiling salted water for about 3 minutes, or until tender. Drain well, add lemon juice, mix and set aside.
3. Melt butter in large skillet, add garlic and shallots and cook for 3 minutes. Add mushrooms and cook an additional 5 minutes. Add brandy and cream and bring mixture to boil.
4. Remove from heat and add spaghetti squash and spinach. Salt and pepper liberally and add bacon bits and parmesan cheese. Toss well.
5. Place two spinach leaves on each serving plate and top with spaghetti squash mixture. Serve immediately.
SPAGHETTI SQUASH REMOULADE
Four servings
Preparation time: 20 minutes
Cooking time: 20 minutes
1 spaghetti squash
1 teaspoon lemon juice
