Fewer than half of Regina residents trust City Hall, according to a citizen satisfaction survey conducted by the municipality.
Mayor Michael Fougere considers that 43-per-cent figure the most concerning result of the survey, which polled nearly 800 residents last year.
Perhaps relatedly, while nine in 10 residents are interested in knowing how their property tax dollars are spent, just over half say they understand how that works and that the city does a good job at informing residents what their bills are contributing to.
��The trust, I think, is an indicator that we need to do a better job of informing residents, of telling the story about the city,�� said city manager Chris Holden.
Going forward, he said the city will focus on improved communication with residents, especially with regard to taxes, and more opportunities for citizens to participate in government. A review of the city��s citizen advisory committees, for instance, is due back next year.
Holden added the city plans to improve customer service, and accountability and reporting.
While the trust statistic doesn��t shine brightly on the city, Fougere said it is important to consider it alongside other survey results: That most residents believe quality of life in the city is good and the municipality is on track to improve Regina in the next decade. Plus, most say they��re proud to live in Regina.
Also, surprisingly to the mayor, more than half of residents would support a property tax increase if necessary to maintain or improve services, though a hike to user fees is preferred. About half, meanwhile, also think they get good value for their taxes.
Infrastructure, traffic and roads dominates the priority list for residents, followed by crime, safety and policing, and homelessness, poverty and affordable housing{g}.{g}
