Released funds can help to fund family holiday[GETTY]
While many people dream of relaxing once they have stopped working, this is simply not an option for those who have credit cards and loans to clear, or interest-only mortgages without sufficient funds in place to repay the capital. However, the good news is you may be able to clear all these debts through a scheme known as equity release; this allows people to draw on their housing equity to boost their finances in later life. In fact, new research from over-55s' adviser, Key Retirement Solutions, shows substantial numbers of retired homeowners are now releasing money from their properties in this way to clear debts. According to its findings, 20 per cent used some or all of the money to pay off mortgages, while 30 per cent are clearing credit cards or loans. "Debt is one of the biggest issues for people in retirement," says Dean Mirfin from Key. "This may be due to outstanding card or loan debts, an endowment shortfall, or an interestonly mortgage where there is no repayment scheme. "For those who might otherwise have no option but to sell their home and downsize to pay off these debts, equity release can offer an alternative. "It can help people clear what they owe without the stress of having to move to a new, smaller property and can enable retirees to enjoy their later years without having debts hanging over them." SO HOW DOES IT WORK? An equity release scheme allows you to unlock the cash tied up in your bricks and mortar while still enabling you to live in your home. You can do this either with a tax-free lump sum or by opting for a drawdown product that offers smaller amounts of income in regular instalments when you need them.
We are witnessing record growth as more asset-rich over-55s take advantage of products that can providethem with security and stability Nigel Waterson, Equity Release Council chairman
Typically, you don't have to make any monthly payments while you are alive, as the amount you owe plus the interest is paid back when your house is sold. This tends to happen when you pass away - or when you move into long-term care. If there is money left over from the sale, this then goes to your loved ones as an inheritance in the usual way. As house prices continue to soar upwards, with the average British home increasing in value by £90 a day over the first half of 2014 and now standing at £260,488 (up 6.5 per cent compared to January) according to property site Zoopla, more pensioner homeowners have large amounts of equity at their disposal. THE MARKET CONTINUES TO BE{r} A{g}VA{r}IL{g}ABLE{r}
