Helpless. That is how they looked. Like little boys, who knew they had done something horribly wrong, and who desperately wanted the whole sad sickening mess to disappear.
But nothing disappears in the age of ten million camera angles and super slow-motion replay. Especially not if the sickening mess is a catastrophic mistake on the World Cup stage.
Soccer��s grand showcase is less than a week old. Already some of the player��s performances have been memorable — for all the wrong reasons.
England��s goalkeeper, Robert Green, should consider moving to Greenland and changing his name after botching a save a toddler could have made in a 1-1 tie with the United States. Zdravko Kuzmanovic��s ill-timed handball sank Serbia against Ghana. Denmark��s Simon Poulsen headed the ball into his own net in a 2-0 loss to the Netherlands.
The games keep coming. So do the gaffes. And soccer fans will not forget them. They are an unforgiving bunch, as are the British tabloids. The London rags pummeled England��s ham-handed Mr. Green, slaughtering him with an assortment of nasty headlines.
Among the best: ��Tainted Glove.�� ��Green Fingers. �� ��Hand of Clod.��
Mr. Green��s teammates stood by their besieged brother, saying ��mistakes happen�� — and can happen to anyone. Here is the thing: the person they happen to is the one who has to live with the consequences.
